WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.949
Development, test, staging, and production should have distinct configuration expectations.

2
00:00:05.949 --> 00:00:11.767
Development often needs local endpoints, sample data, detailed errors, and fast feedback.

3
00:00:11.767 --> 00:00:22.881
Production needs controlled diagnostics, protected secrets, approved integrations, reliable identity settings, and behavior that matches user and compliance expectations.

4
00:00:22.881 --> 00:00:26.934
Development settings should not silently travel to production.

5
00:00:26.934 --> 00:00:40.139
Debug behavior, detailed errors, sample accounts, mock services, local credentials, permissive policies, and temporary flags should be contained by environment-specific configuration and release review.

6
00:00:40.139 --> 00:00:45.434
A setting that is useful during development can be risky in a public environment.

7
00:00:45.434 --> 00:00:49.749
Deployment profiles and slots can act as configuration boundaries.

8
00:00:49.749 --> 00:00:58.117
They let teams separate staging validation from production rollout, but they also create another place where settings can drift.

9
00:00:58.117 --> 00:01:05.504
Slot swaps, copied profiles, and older templates should be reviewed so production does not inherit test behavior.

10
00:01:05.504 --> 00:01:09.165
Environment naming should be consistent and unambiguous.

11
00:01:09.165 --> 00:01:16.748
The application should not depend on unclear strings or ad hoc checks to decide whether it is running in production.

12
00:01:16.748 --> 00:01:23.743
If environment detection fails or is ambiguous, the safe response should not be to enable relaxed behavior.

13
00:01:23.743 --> 00:01:28.188
Production configuration changes should require review and approval.

14
00:01:28.188 --> 00:01:35.314
Emergency edits may be necessary, but they should still be tracked, owned, and reconciled after the incident.

15
00:01:35.314 --> 00:01:42.439
Unreviewed temporary settings are one of the easiest ways for a secure deployment to become unsafe over time.
